WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.000
Something has to clear the potassium from the space outside neurons.

2
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:08.980
Who does it, and how?

3
00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:15.830
In 1966, Stephen Kuffler, John Nicholls and Richard Orkand recorded from glial

4
00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:16.830
cells.

5
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:20.513
They worked in the optic nerve of an amphibian, the mudpuppy

6
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:22.767
Read the record first.

7
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.467
The glial cell's voltage sits steady and very negative.

8
00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:30.527
Then the neighbouring axons fire a train of impulses.

9
00:00:31.507 --> 00:00:33.507
The glial cell slowly depolarizes.

10
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.290
Longer and faster trains depolarize it more.

11
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.690
When the axons stop, it recovers over seconds.

12
00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:43.383
What is the glial cell sensing?

13
00:00:49.483 --> 00:00:55.663
Potassium. Changing the potassium in the bath moved the glial voltage as a potassium

14
00:00:55.663 --> 00:00:56.663
electrode's would.

15
00:00:57.583 --> 00:01:02.103
So the glia were sensing the potassium the axons released as it built up.

16
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:07.517
Astrocytes are joined by gap junctions, channels that link the insides of

17
00:01:07.517 --> 00:01:08.517
neighbouring cells.

18
00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:12.037
Their membranes are almost purely permeable to potassium.

19
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:18.210
In this simplified, strongly coupled network, the astrocytes stay at nearly the same

20
00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:23.550
voltage. What differs from place to place is the local E_K, set by the local

21
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.550
potassium.

22
00:01:25.183 --> 00:01:27.003
Now let one spot fire hard.

23
00:01:27.923 --> 00:01:31.343
There, the local E_K is more positive than the network's voltage.

24
00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:34.043
So potassium flows in.

25
00:01:34.863 --> 00:01:39.023
In this model it enters through the potassium channel Kir4.1.

26
00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:42.703
That entry depolarizes the network slightly.

27
00:01:43.463 --> 00:01:46.443
Where potassium is still low, potassium now flows out.

28
00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:51.143
Entry and exit happen at the same time, as one loop of current.

29
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:53.243
This is spatial buffering.

30
00:01:54.183 --> 00:01:57.203
It moves potassium from where it is high to where it is low.

31
00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:01.503
On balance, the potassium stays outside, only spread thinner.

32
00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:05.407
Putting it back inside cells is a separate job.

33
00:02:06.487 --> 00:02:10.427
Sodium-potassium pumps in astrocytes and neurons take potassium up.

34
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.430
How much each mechanism contributes is still debated.

35
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.910
Ken McCarthy's laboratory deleted Kir4.1 from glia in mice.

36
00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:25.703
In hippocampal slices, astrocytes lacking the channel took up potassium poorly.

37
00:02:26.783 --> 00:02:29.663
What else might fail when this potassium channel is gone?

38
00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:39.807
Glutamate uptake. That does not mean the channel carries glutamate.

39
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:43.667
Glutamate transport depends on the astrocyte's voltage.

40
00:02:44.717 --> 00:02:48.017
And Kir4.1 helps hold that voltage negative.

41
00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:55.070
One limit. The deletion began in development, and it also reached oligodendrocytes.

42
00:02:55.933 --> 00:02:59.233
So the result cannot be pinned on mature astrocytes alone.

43
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:03.357
So astrocytes sense the potassium that firing releases.

44
00:03:04.257 --> 00:03:07.797
They spread it through their network, and pumps take it back inside cells.
